    "updated_at": "2023-10-24T08:23:52Z",
    "body": "This removes several things which are relevant to web authors as conformance requirements and semantics:\r\n\r\n- \"Ignored segments do not form part of the document's semantics\"\r\n- \"Base text segments can overlap (with a limit of...)\"\r\n- \"In a conforming document, each base text segment is associated with at least one annotation segment, and each annotation segment is associated with one base text segment.\"\r\n- \"A [ruby](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-ruby-element) element [represents](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#represents) the union of the segments of base text it contains, along with the mapping from those base text segments to annotation segments.\"\r\n\r\nI'm unsure what we should do here. At the very least, we need to have something which the `ruby` element \"represents\". And we should keep the conformance requirements as well; for example, they're what makes it invalid HTML to just write `<ruby><rt>foo</rt></ruby>` (violating \"each annotation segment is associated with one base text segment\").\r\n\r\nYou said that CSS Ruby defines how to handle these CSS properties. Does it also give a way of determining the semantics, in terms of base text and mapping of base text segments to annotation segments? I tried to look through it and didn't find any algorithm of similar detail to this one. There is some talk of \"ruby bases\", but it seems to assume the existence of `display: ruby-base`, which is a single-implementer feature. Does it define segmentation at all?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
